  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving Multi-Hazard Early-Warning and Preparedness Efforts in Disaster and Conflict Affected Areas in Somalia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia is at risk of several natural hazards, including drought, floods, cyclones, and climate-related diseases and epidemics, with continued and lasting effects of conflict and violence. This has led to 8.25 million people in need of humanitarian assistance (Somalia HRP 2023), with more than 4 million of them displaced internally by conflict, insecurity, forced evictions, drought, and floods &#x2013; around 30 per cent of the country&apos;s total population (IOM Displacement Tracking Matrix). The Somali population therefore has pre-existing vulnerabilities related to high poverty rates and is experiencing the compounded effects of long-term conflict and recurrent natural disasters. In 2023, Somalia faced its worst drought for at least 40 years. This crisis has combined with flooding and ongoing security threats posed by extremist non-state actors to cause one of the world&apos;s largest displacement crises.With the main objective of contributing to improved resilience for vulnerable communities affected by conflict and recurrent disasters, IOM proposes an integrated intervention to strengthen the capacity of response systems, national and local institutions, and communities themselves. The intervention focuses on locations in Bay, Bakool and Gedo that are likely to be impacted by multiple hazards, including the planned Government offensive against Al-Shabab.</narrative>
  </description>
